course: dictionary
lesson: 7131
title: Meaning from a clever word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are explained by the words around them. «A swing is a pendulum: a weight hanging from a point, going back and forth with its own steady rhythm.» The sentence gives you the clue: a swing is a pendulum, and the words back and forth and rhythm tell you it moves in a steady, repeated way.
explain: «A grandfather clock counts the swings of its pendulum.» Ask yourself what word would fit in the gap, and then try your meaning in the sentence. A pendulum is the part that swings, and its swinging keeps the clock counting time.
explain: Words can also stretch to new meanings. The «pendulum of opinion» is not about a clock at all. It means that people's ideas can swing from one extreme to the other and back again, just as a real pendulum swings from side to side.
fact: A pendulum is a weight hanging from a fixed point on a string or rod, which swings backwards and forwards.
fact: «A pendulum» can also be the swinging part of an old clock, like a grandfather clock, which keeps the clock on time.
fact: «A short pendulum swings quickly and a long one slowly»; one four times as long takes twice as long to swing.
fact: «A pendulum about 1 metre long takes about 1 second to swing one way.»
fact: The idea of a pendulum can also mean something changing from one extreme to the other and back again, like the «pendulum of opinion».
exercise: «A swing is a pendulum: a weight hanging from a point, going back and forth.» What is a pendulum? | *A weight hanging from a point that swings to and fro | A kind of bridge over a river | A type of cloud
exercise: «A short pendulum swings quickly and a long one slowly.» Which pendulum swings more slowly? | A short pendulum | Both swing at exactly the same speed | A pendulum with no string | *A long pendulum
exercise: «The pendulum of opinion» describes ideas that do what? | Get heavier each day | Stay still forever | *Swing from one extreme to the other and back again
test: «Grandfather clocks keep time with a swinging part.» What is that swinging part called? | A dial | A spring | *A pendulum | A hammer
test: «Moving the weight up makes the pendulum shorter, so the clock runs faster.» If a pendulum gets shorter, what happens to the clock? | It runs slower | It stops | It becomes louder | *It runs faster
exam: «One four times as long takes twice as long to swing.» If one pendulum is four times as long as another, how long does it take to swing compared with the shorter one? | The same time | Four times as long | Half as long | *Twice as long
